title: '2026-11-13: the account of what each session and subagent cost is built, and runs with ''python3 tools/team/spending_account.py''.'
pid: 8f5f8969-c980-4259-b180-4253a5271cfc
body: |2-
  What was done. 'tools/team/spending_account.py' reads every transcript
  under 'my-dot-claude/projects/' and brings
  'data/claude/spending/account.tsv' up to date. That file is
  tab-separated with a header, and has one row per session and one per
  subagent (138 now). Its '--help' says what each column is and how to
  query it with awk or sqlite3. The run takes half a second. Running it
  again replaces the rows of transcripts that still exist and keeps the
  rest, so no row is doubled or lost. Beyond the columns asked for, a row has 'parent' (for
  nested subagents), 'title' and 'usd'. The test,
  'tools/team/test_spending_account.py', runs on transcripts it makes.
  Nothing in 'bash/all-tests.sh' ran Python, so I added a "Team tool
  tests" phase that runs every 'tools/team/test_*.py' (33 lines). The
  whole suite passed, all 72 integration tests included.
  How I checked it. The pacer's log names 34 sessions, ten of them on 2026-11-13. Each
  matches exactly one row whose origin is 'pacer': its start is within a second of the log's,
  and so is its end, now that a session ends when its last subagent ends. No
  pacer row is left unmatched. Token sums equal Claude Code's own
  'cost-state' records model by model, and so do the dollars.
  The one comparable number is 'usd': each request priced at the list
  prices Claude Code itself applies. It is honest as an API price. Whether the
  subscription's limits weigh tokens the same way is not published.
  Scarlett could test that by comparing each pacer interval's rise in
  the week's fraction (pacer log) with the sum of 'usd' in that interval.
  What I am unsure of. The price of an hour's cache write for Sonnet and
  Haiku is assumed, since no such write has happened yet. I have not seen the 30-day
  transcript deletion happen. 'origin' 'jeff' means any interactive
  terminal session.
  What surprised me. Per token, Fable costs twice what Opus costs, and the pacer runs
  Top on Fable, so a pacer session of Top ($1 to $3) costs about as much
  as a specialist's mission. Three missions to Mario were written in
  Top's own words, not in 'specialist.sh prompt's, so the account
  cannot say they were Mario's ($6.61 together). Lend every mind through
  that prompt and sums by specialist come out whole.
  Suggestions. If the pacer ran the script after each session (one line in
  'session ()' of 'tools/team/pacer.sh'), the account would stay current, and
  Claude Code's 30-day deletion could not take a transcript before its
  row is written. Scarlett, once born, is the natural owner of both. The
  account holds the first lines of Jeff's own prompts, some private (medical
  records). It is under 'data/', which git ignores; keep it out of any
  public skgrepo.
  Nothing is committed: the two new scripts, the change to
  'bash/all-tests.sh', and the notes under my "what I know".
